**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 11 in full; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), the complete solutions unit; [Química geral no laboratório — Marcela Boroski, organizer](../sources/Quimica-geral-no-laboratorio.lingua#r_48YEN2SA34DW0FPCSTMVTW3CTD), the corresponding solution/equilibrium activities.

Solution formation combines disruption and formation of interactions, molecular/ionic arrangements, and entropy changes. A useful qualitative “like dissolves like” rule needs a physical account and can fail outside its intended range. Specify solvent, solute, composition, temperature, pressure, and relevant chemical speciation.

Solubility is an equilibrium property under stated conditions. Dissolution rate, particle size, stirring, wetting, and transport affect how quickly an observation approaches a state; they do not automatically change the final equilibrium solubility of the same bulk phases. Reactions, complexation, polymorphs, or nanoscale effects can change which system is actually being compared.

Distinguish saturated, unsaturated, and supersaturated descriptions with respect to a specified phase. An apparently clear solution may be metastable, and a visible solid may contain several phases. A total elemental concentration can combine free ions, complexes, and other dissolved species; later equilibrium work makes that partition quantitative.

**Work:** compare identified textbook solubility cases and explain the competing contributions. Design a paper study that separates rate from equilibrium, including temperature control and evidence that the solid phase is the intended one. Translate the same composition between amount concentration, molality, and mass fraction with its required density information.

Complete the whole solutions/colloids chapter and corresponding Chem1 unit. Return to the shared concentration Record whenever a denominator becomes ambiguous. The later acid/base, solubility, extraction, and water studies reuse this account rather than treating dissolved matter as a new unrelated subject.

